    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions",
    "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨1-3{t}^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$",
    "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
